---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { release113 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="skills-and-commands">

          <p>
            A skill is a packaged set of instructions for a recurring kind of work — a house style, a review checklist,
            a deployment runbook. A custom command is a prompt template you keep as a Markdown file.
          </p>

          <h2 id="skills">Skills</h2>
          <p>
            Vector's agents find a skill as a <code>SKILL.md</code>, in its own folder, in these places: a
            <code>skill/</code> or <code>skills/</code> folder of the repository's <code>.vector</code> folder or of your
            global config, <code>.claude/skills</code> and <code>.agents/skills</code> in your home folder and in the
            repository, the folders your config lists under <code>skills.paths</code>, and the addresses under
            <code>skills.urls</code>, which Vector downloads. A loose <code>&lt;name&gt;.md</code> beside them is not a
            skill to these agents; only the separate V2 session runtime's catalog reads one. The agent calls a skill by
            name from the list of available skills.
          </p>
          <p>
            Skills a repository ships load only once you trust its settings, as its other settings do. Downloaded skills
            are cached under a versioned directory, so updating one is clean rather than a manual copy. Skills are a
            Vector mechanism, not an MCP server.
          </p>

          <h2 id="learned">Learned skills</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} Vector can also draft a skill from your own runs: when a run turns a failing check green,
            it keeps the steps that worked for this repository, in <code>brain.db</code> on this computer rather than as a
            file. A draft waits for your review in Settings → Memory → Skills, and the agent sees a learned skill only
            once it is in use. A learned skill is guidance the agent may load, never a rule. A disk or built-in skill of
            the same name wins. See <a href="/docs/brain#learned-skills">Learned skills</a> in Vector Brain.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} loading any skill asks the <code>skill</code> permission, as your rules set it. A learned
            skill that Vector put in use on its own, after a second matching success with Apply automatically on, also
            asks <code>learned_skill</code> before it loads; one you approved does not. Every command in a skill still
            goes through your permission rules.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a learned skill reaches your team only when you choose <strong>Share to
            repository</strong> in Settings → Memory → Skills, or run <code>vector memory skills share</code> in a
            terminal outside Vector. Sharing writes one file in <code>.vector/skills/</code> that you commit yourself. It
            carries no error excerpt, session ids, stats or evidence, and Vector checks it against your personal memory
            first. The file is a skill like any other in the repository, so it changes the repository's settings: Vector
            asks you to trust them again once your running sessions finish, and from the terminal you approve them with
            <code>vector trust approve</code> in a terminal outside Vector.
          </p>

          <h2 id="commands">Custom commands</h2>
          <p>
            Your own commands are Markdown files in a <code>command/</code> or <code>commands/</code> folder, in the repository or in your global config. Frontmatter carries the metadata, the body is the prompt template, and the file's path becomes the name — so <code>command/review/api.md</code> is available as <code>review/api</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector run --command review/api "the new billing endpoints"</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
</DocsPage>
